<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    The launcher's own Fluent 2 dialect. Everything here is either an x:Key'd ControlTheme or a
    class selector (.tm-*), so it is opt-in and cannot leak into the settings window or the shared
    message box, both of which stay on Avalonia's own control themes by design.

    There is deliberately NOT a single colour literal in this file — every brush comes from
    Themes/Fluent2/Tokens.axaml, and Fluent2TokenTests fails the build if one appears. That is what
    makes light/dark a property of one file instead of a hunt.

    All brush references use {DynamicResource}, not {StaticResource}: the app switches
    ThemeVariant live when the OS theme or the appearance setting changes, and a StaticResource
    would freeze the launcher in whatever variant happened to be active at parse time.
  -->

  <Styles.Resources>

    <!-- ══ Launcher item tile ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The most-repeated control in the app, and — per the round-2 visual-pass review — a real
         Fluent 2 STANDARD BUTTON now rather than a borderless icon+text row: a subtle fill
         (SurfaceLayerBrush, the same layered surface Border.tm-card and the search field use), a
         hairline border (StrokeControlBrush) and the shape scale's 4 px control radius, all visible
         AT REST rather than appearing only on hover. TmButton below gives every other button in the
         app (the settings-card actions) that same look; this theme duplicates its template rather
         than deriving from it via BasedOn so a tile's own quirks — Stretch content alignment for the
         icon+label DockPanel, its own fixed Padding, and disabling by making the WHOLE card recede to
         nothing rather than just dimming it — stay self-contained. Unlike the retro build, pressing
         does NOT offset the content — Fluent 2 signals press by going a shade quieter, not by moving.

         There is deliberately NO Width Setter here any more. Round 3, Task 4 removed the 小/中/大
         button-size setting and left every tile at one fixed 168 DIP; Round 4, item 3 deleted that
         fixed width too, because it was what forced long labels through TextTrimming. A tile is now
         as wide as its own content: 2 DIP of border + 16 DIP of Padding (both below) + the 20 DIP
         icon box (Image.tm-tile-icon further down) + the icon's own 10 DIP right margin
         (Views/AppPage.axaml) + the label's single-line advance = 48 DIP of chrome plus the label.

         What keeps that from reading as a ragged right edge is the ABSENCE of a HorizontalAlignment
         on the tile Button, so do not add one: each tile stretches to the width of the column
         StackPanel it sits in, and a vertical StackPanel is as wide as its widest child, so a column
         comes out uniform and only differs from the next column. Measured on the running build with
         UI Automation bounding rectangles (Round 4, item 3, on a 74-item folder): exactly one width
         and one right edge per column, and every one of the nine columns a different width, from
         118.67 to 204 DIP.

         A MinWidth and a MaxWidth are equally deliberately absent: MaxWidth would bring the ellipsis
         straight back, and a MinWidth floor would only re-pad the columns that came out short. The
         bound that matters is applied where the screen is actually visible — WindowPlacement clamps
         the fitted window to the work area and AppPage's Body ScrollViewer scrolls whatever is past
         it. Both were exercised at 每列个数 = 2, where the page asked for 5644 DIP, the window was
         clamped to the 5120 DIP work area, and Body reported itself horizontally scrollable. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmTileButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,7" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />

      <!-- An adorner rather than a border: it draws OUTSIDE the tile, so showing focus cannot
           reflow the grid the way a border thickness change would. -->
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <!-- Windows 11's two-ring focus rectangle: a 2 px outer ring in the text colour with a
               1 px inner ring in the opposite colour, so it reads against both a light tile and a
               dark one without knowing which it is over. -->
          <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="2"
                  CornerRadius="7"
                  Margin="-3">
            <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeInnerBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="1"
                    CornerRadius="5" />
          </Border>
        </Template>
      </Setter>

      <!-- Two layers: Root paints the persistent fill/border and StateLayer paints ONLY the
           hover/pressed wash on top of it. One layer cannot do both — swapping Root's own Background
           straight from SurfaceLayerBrush to SubtleFillSecondaryBrush on hover would make the tile
           LESS opaque, not more, because those Subtle* alphas are calibrated to sit on a transparent
           rest state (see TmIconButton below), not to stack visibly over a fill that is already
           there. StateLayer's own CornerRadius is RadiusControlInner — RadiusControl minus one — so it
           sits just inside Root's hairline border instead of its square corners poking out past Root's
           rounded ones. It was a hardcoded 3 in this template and in TmButton's below until Tokens.axaml
           grew that token; see its own comment there for why the derived value has to be a second
           literal and why it is declared next to the one it derives from. -->
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border x:Name="Root"
                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                  CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
            <Border x:Name="StateLayer" Background="Transparent" CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControlInner}">
              <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                                VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
            </Border>
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>

      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#StateLayer">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#StateLayer">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillTertiaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- A tile whose shortcut target no longer exists. It stays in place and stays readable —
           it is information, not noise — but it takes no pointer states and cannot be launched, and
           the card recedes to nothing (fill AND border both go transparent) rather than merely
           dimming, so it reads as "not a button" instead of "a greyed-out button". -->
      <Style Selector="^:disabled">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:disabled /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      </Style>
      <!-- The disabled tile's ICON is dimmed by a rule in the Styles body below, not here: a
           ControlTheme may not contain a descendant selector, and the icon lives in the launcher's
           DataTemplate rather than in this control template. -->
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Header icon button (☰ nav toggle, ✕ close) ═══════════════════════════════════════════
         A 32×32 square target. Same subtle-fill state model as the tile so the whole surface reads
         as one system. (Round 3, Task 2 removed this theme's third user, the title strip's own ⋯
         overflow button — MainWindow.axaml's rail comment explains where its functions went.) -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmIconButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="2" CornerRadius="6" Margin="-2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border x:Name="Root"
                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillTertiaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Standard button (settings-card actions) ════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Fluent 2's control-button surface, used everywhere a settings card needs an actual button
         (打开/更改…/重置为默认/清理缓存/备份…/恢复…/固定到任务栏/清除/检查更新/下载更新, …), replacing
         Avalonia's own default Fluent-1 Button look those rows rendered with before. 32 px tall at
         100% scaling — MinHeight rather than a fixed Height, so the actual on-screen height still
         tracks the OS text-scale setting the way every other control here does, instead of being a
         hardcoded pixel box — with the same fill/border/radius/state-layer shape TmTileButton uses
         above. See that theme's comment for why the hover/pressed wash is a second layer rather than
         a straight Background swap, and why the two themes duplicate the template instead of one
         deriving from the other via BasedOn.

         Round 7, Task 3 made this the ONLY settings-card button theme. TmAccentButton — a BasedOn
         derivative that swapped the rest-state fill and the text colour to the accent pair — was
         deleted with the Aero control themes, because Windows 7 had no accent-filled default button
         to imitate: the Aero command button is the gradient one and there is exactly one of it. Its
         three call sites (AppPage's PinNowButton, UpdatesCard's UpdateActionBtn, and the run-time
         swap in BehaviorCard.axaml.cs's ApplyTaskbarPinState) all landed here, and each one's own
         comment records what it gave up. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,0" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="2" CornerRadius="7" Margin="-3">
            <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeInnerBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="5" />
          </Border>
        </Template>
      </Setter>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border x:Name="Root"
                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                  CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
            <Border x:Name="StateLayer" Background="Transparent" CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControlInner}">
              <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                                VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
            </Border>
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#StateLayer">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#StateLayer">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillTertiaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:disabled">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:disabled /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.6" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Nav rail command row ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Round 3, Task 2: 打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出 moved out of the title strip's ⋯ overflow flyout
         into their own rows in the navigation rail's bottom-pinned footer (MainWindow.axaml). Three of
         those four are this theme's consumers today — Round 5, item 2 took 关于 out of the rail and made
         it a settings card, and its row went with it. Padding
         (12,10) and Margin (4,2) are IDENTICAL to TmNavRowItem below — a command row
         has to measure and sit exactly like a page row for the rail to read as one consistent list —
         but this is a full custom Button ControlTheme, not that ListBoxItem theme reused on a Button,
         because a Button has NO :selected pseudoclass at all: that is what makes "a command row must
         never look selected" (the task's own constraint) true by construction rather than by a style
         rule a later edit could defeat. Same two-layer Root/nothing-else shape as TmIconButton (a
         command row needs no separate state layer the way TmTileButton/TmButton do, since it has no
         persistent rest-state fill to protect from being replaced outright by the hover wash — Root
         IS transparent at rest). -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmNavRowButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,10" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="4,2" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="2" CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" Margin="-2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border x:Name="Root"
                  Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillTertiaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!-- ══ Nav rail page row ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The other rail row kind: a DESTINATION the window navigates to and stays on (应用, 设置), so
         unlike TmNavRowButton above it is a ListBoxItem and it has a :selected state. The two live
         side by side here because they must measure alike — Padding (12,10) and Margin (4,2) are the
         same literals in both, which is what makes the rail read as one list.

         Round 6, item 3 replaced what used to be three plain styles keyed off Classes="tm-nav" with
         this ControlTheme, because of ONE property those styles could not express. The old selected
         look was BorderThickness="3,0,0,0" on the stock template's ContentPresenter, and a border
         always runs the full length of the edge AND always consumes layout: it painted the accent
         down the whole 36 DIP row box and it pushed the row's own icon sideways. Both were measured
         against Task Manager's rail at 150% scale, with the two windows' rails occupying the same
         image columns:

           · TM's accent is 24 px tall — 16 DIP — and centred on the row box, not the full row.
           · TM's accent is a PILL: 5 px wide through the middle, narrowing to 3 px on its first and
             last scanlines, i.e. a cap radius near half its width.
           · TM's glyph does not move when the row is selected. Driving SelectionItemPattern.Select()
             on its 性能 row and re-capturing, the 进程 glyph's ink sits at the same x in both states.
             Ours moved its icon TextBlock 2 px (1.33 DIP) to the right on selection — the border's
             layout cost, reported by UI Automation on the icon itself.

         Everything the measurements found IDENTICAL is deliberately untouched: the 48 DIP rail pane,
         the 40x36 DIP row box, the 40 DIP row pitch, the 4 DIP corner arc, the empty unselected row,
         and the accent hue — the two apps' indicators sampled the same triple in each variant, so
         AccentDefaultBrush (which AppTheme republishes from Windows personalization at startup) is
         already right and is carried over unchanged.

         So the indicator is an element, not a border: a 3x16 DIP Border with CornerRadius 1.5, laid
         over the content in a Panel, hit-test invisible, transparent until :selected paints it. The
         three numbers stay literals rather than becoming tokens, following Border.tm-nav-separator's
         precedent further down — they are one indivisible measured shape, not a step on a scale.
         Width stays the 3 DIP this file has always authored, and moving from a BorderThickness to an
         element did shift the render by a pixel: measured at 150%, ours was 4 px wide before this
         change and is 5 px now — the same 5 px TM draws — from an authored number that never moved.
         3 DIP at 150% is 4.5, so layout rounding covers 4 and 5 alike and pixels cannot separate a
         3.0 from a 3.33. Do NOT "correct" the width to 3.33 to chase TM's 5 px: it would be wrong at
         every other scale (the same argument Round 4 used for the 1 DIP pane border). Height 16 is
         TM's 24 px / 1.5. The radius is half the width; TM's measured cap radius is about 1.67 DIP.

         The selected fill moved from SubtleFillTertiaryBrush to SubtleFillSecondaryBrush, and that is
         a LOOKUP, not a taste call: TM's selected row solves to alpha 9.4/255 black in Light and
         14.5/255 white in Dark against its own backdrop in the same capture, and this app's
         SubtleFillSecondaryBrush is exactly 10/255 and 15/255 (Tokens.axaml). Ours was 6.4 and 11.3,
         i.e. SubtleFillTertiaryBrush. A real consequence, accepted: :pointerover already uses that
         same brush, so a hovered unselected row and a resting selected row now match, and the pill
         alone carries selection — which is exactly what TM does, and it also fixes an inversion this
         file used to have, where hover was STRONGER than selection.

         Hover is otherwise untouched. TM's hover and press states cannot be measured in this
         environment at all (no pointer), and the rule is not to change a property no difference was
         measured in. :pressed and the FocusAdorner are copied verbatim from TmNavRowButton for a
         different reason: replacing a template replaces its focus visual and its state styles too,
         and dropping a state silently while swapping a template is not a match-Task-Manager change,
         it is a regression. There is no ^:selected:pointerover rule because after this change both
         states resolve to the same brush already. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmNavRowItem" TargetType="ListBoxItem">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,10" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="4,2" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="FocusAdorner">
        <Template>
          <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource FocusStrokeOuterBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="2" CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" Margin="-2" />
        </Template>
      </Setter>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Panel>
            <Border x:Name="Root"
                    Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                    CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}" />
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}" />
            <Border x:Name="SelectionIndicator"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    Width="3" Height="16" CornerRadius="1.5"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False" />
          </Panel>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillTertiaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:selected /template/ Border#Root">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:selected /template/ Border#SelectionIndicator">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AccentDefaultBrush}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

  </Styles.Resources>

  <!-- ══ Search header ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 7, Task 1 restored this rule along with the field it frames. It was deleted by commit
       6d00060, which moved search into the window's title strip; the Aero conversion deletes that strip
       (win-window-frames: "Don't add controls to a window frame. Put the controls within the window
       instead.") and the header comes back to Views/AppPage.axaml with it.

       The three values are the ones the rule shipped with before 6d00060 and are unchanged by the
       revert: a 1 DIP bottom divider and 12,10 of padding. Their Aero re-derivation — the divider
       becomes a group-box hairline and the padding becomes the published 11 relative-pixel dialog
       margin — belongs to Task 9, which is where the field's own Aero treatment lands. -->

  <Style Selector="Border.tm-header">
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeDividerBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,10" />
  </Style>


  <!-- Iconography is Segoe Fluent Icons — the Windows 11 icon face — rather than hand-drawn
       geometry, so the launcher's search, overflow and close glyphs are the same shapes the shell
       and every other Windows 11 app draw. Segoe MDL2 Assets stands in on Windows 10 at the same
       code points. Both are system fonts; nothing is bundled. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-icon">
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource IconFontFamily}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="16" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Quieter icons: the search affordance and the folder hint are labels, not actions. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-icon.subtle">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextTertiaryBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-icon.small">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The search field is a plain TextBox stripped to text only; the field's own shape, fill and
       outline are drawn by the Border it sits in (Border.tm-searchfield). Background and
       BorderThickness are set as LOCAL values on the element in Views/AppPage.axaml — local values
       outrank style setters, which is what keeps Avalonia's own :pointerover / :focus TextBox
       styling from repainting a field we already painted. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,0" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TextTertiaryBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Round 3, Task 1 (user item 2): Windows 11's own TextBox focus is NOT a full accent ring — it
       keeps its plain 1 px resting border on all four sides (Border.tm-searchfield's own BorderBrush/
       BorderThickness above are left untouched by this rule, unlike the old version, which repainted
       BorderBrush to AccentDefaultBrush and drew an accent rectangle around the whole field) and only
       the fill lightens slightly, to SurfaceLayerActiveBrush (Tokens.axaml). The 2 px accent UNDERLINE
       on the bottom edge only is a separate element — Border.tm-searchfield-focusbar, added inside
       SearchField in Views/AppPage.axaml — because a Border cannot draw a differently-coloured thickness
       on one side while keeping the other three at the ambient resting colour: BorderBrush is a single
       brush for every side. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield.focused">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerActiveBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The bar itself: transparent at rest, so it takes no visual space and needs no separate
       Opacity/IsVisible toggle. IsHitTestVisible="False" (set in Views/AppPage.axaml, not here — a
       ControlTheme/Style cannot express it any earlier than the element's own local markup) keeps it
       from ever intercepting a click meant for the TextBox underneath. CornerRadius "0,0,3,3" is
       RadiusControlInner (RadiusControl minus one) on the bottom corners only, and Margin "1,0,1,1"
       insets it a hairline inside SearchField's own 1 px border and radius — the same "sit just inside
       the parent's border" idea RadiusControlInner already documents in Tokens.axaml, reused here
       instead of a second literal. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield-focusbar">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-searchfield.focused Border.tm-searchfield-focusbar">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AccentDefaultBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- There is deliberately NO :disabled rule for this field. Round 6, item 1 established that on a
       page that cannot be searched the field is not shown at all rather than greyed out; Round 7,
       Task 1 made that structural — the field is inside the 应用 page now, so a page that is not 应用
       simply does not contain it and MainWindow's old "SearchField.IsVisible = …" write is deleted.
       Either way nothing in this app puts this field into :disabled and a rule for that state would
       have no producer left. -->

  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-search">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="CaretBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="30" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
  </Style>
  <!-- There is deliberately no ":disabled" companion to the rule above either. It used to repaint
       Foreground and PlaceholderForeground to TextDisabledBrush, because the Foreground set above at
       Style precedence outranks the Fluent TextBox ControlTheme's own built-in :disabled foreground
       and so left the text at full contrast. A page that cannot be searched does not contain this
       field at all any more (Round 6 item 1's decision, made structural by Round 7 Task 1), so nothing
       in this app puts this TextBox into :disabled. Avalonia's own Fluent :disabled styling for TextBox — which also repaints
       Border#PART_BorderElement with a grey slab, and which this file never overrode — goes with it. -->
  <!-- Round 3, Task 1 (user item 2): the actual source of the old "heavy accent rectangle" was never
       Border.tm-searchfield.focused above — it was Avalonia's OWN default TextBox ControlTheme, whose
       "^:focus /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement" style (Avalonia.Themes.Fluent 12.1.0's
       Controls/TextBox.xaml, the SAME pinned version this app ships) repaints THAT inner template
       part's Background/BorderBrush/BorderThickness to accent on focus. SearchBox's own local
       Background="Transparent" BorderThickness="0" (Views/AppPage.axaml) cannot block this: those locals
       win only for the OUTER TextBox's own properties, while the built-in style targets a DIFFERENT
       object — the named part living inside TextBox's control template — which has no competing local
       value of its own. The fix uses the exact precedence this file relies on wherever it reaches into
       a stock template (TextBox.tm-keycap's own PART_BorderElement rules below are the other case): a
       Style declared here outranks a ControlTheme's own template setter, so re-targeting the SAME part
       name, at the SAME pseudo-class, forces it back to invisible,
       leaving Border.tm-searchfield.focused (this field's own fill/underline) as the ONLY focus signal
       that reaches the screen. Confirmed by pixel-sampling a screenshot before this rule existed: a
       solid accent-coloured band sat exactly over PART_BorderElement's own measured bounds (inset from
       SearchField's outer edges by the icon glyph and Border.tm-searchfield's own Padding), not over
       the whole field. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-search:focus /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Tile icon size ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 3, Task 4 removed the 小/中/大 button-size setting, which used to drive tile width, icon
       box and type size together as three size classes (Panel.size-small/medium/large). This rule is
       what is left of that: one icon box, with no size class left to scope it under.

       Round 4, item 3 then deleted TmTileButton's own Width Setter, which makes this 20x20 more
       load-bearing than it looks — it is no longer just how big the icon draws, it is a term in every
       tile's WIDTH (48 DIP of chrome = 2 border + 16 padding + 20 here + 10 icon margin). TmTileButton
       above still carries the FontSize, the other term the label's advance depends on. Note that this
       is a class-scoped style, i.e. a FrameType.Style frame that a logical-tree detach drops, whereas
       TmTileButton is an element-local theme frame that survives one — see MainWindow.RefitToContent's
       guard, which item 3 made more load-bearing for exactly this reason. -->
  <Style Selector="Image.tm-tile-icon">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="20" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A tile whose target is missing: the label greys out via the ControlTheme, and the icon fades
       with it so the whole tile reads as unavailable rather than half-lit. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.tm-tile:disabled Image.tm-tile-icon">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Sections ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- Body Strong, per the Windows 11 ramp: 14/20 SemiBold. The FACE is deliberately inherited
       rather than pinned to FontFamilyText: a user who picked a font in settings expects it here
       too, and at 14 px the inherited default IS the Text optical size anyway. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-section">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightBody}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A group backed by a real folder is clickable; the built-in 常用工具 group has no Directory and
       stays inert, which is why the affordance is a class the view sets rather than a blanket rule. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4,3" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head.clickable">
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head.clickable:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-section-head.clickable:pointerover TextBlock.tm-folder">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-folder">
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Empty / no-results states ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty">
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="24,32" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty > TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightBody}" />
    <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Avalonia's ContentControl defaults HorizontalContentAlignment to Left, not Center as WPF
       does, so an ordinary Button renders its label against the left edge. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty Button">
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Subtitle, per the Windows 11 ramp: 20/28 SemiBold. The FACE comes from the view model
       (TitleFontFamily), which resolves to Segoe UI Variable Display — the optical size Segoe UI
       Variable is designed for at 18 px and up — only while the user has not chosen a font of
       their own. Pinning it here would silently override that setting. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-empty > TextBlock.title">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeSubtitle}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightSubtitle}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,6" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Update notice ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Fluent 2's InfoBar shape: accent-tinted surface, left glyph, no shouting. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.tm-update">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,9" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Taskbar-pin InfoBar ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Controls/InfoBar's surface. Same subtle-fill-plus-stroke shape as the update notice above,
       but a Border (not a Button) — this bar's actions are its own child buttons, not the whole
       surface. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-infobar">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,10" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ Toast ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       A transient, self-clearing error. Sits in the overlay layer and contributes no layout size,
       so it can never resize the popup. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-toast">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBaseBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeSurfaceFlyoutBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,10" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="16" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Bottom" />
    <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowFlyout}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-toast > TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightBody}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The rail's icon contrast ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 6, item 3, the second half of 导航条中图标的选中状态样式和任务管理器不一样. The row shape is
       in TmNavRowItem (Styles.Resources, above); this is the glyph inside it.

       Measured, both apps, both variants, and identical in the selected and the unselected state in
       each: Task Manager's rail glyphs solve to alpha 0.893 black over their row in Light and reach
       pure white in Dark — which IS this app's TextPrimaryBrush to the level in both (Tokens.axaml).
       Ours were tm-icon's own TextSecondaryBrush: alpha 0.605 in Light, (208,209,210) in Dark. So
       this is a lookup, not a colour choice.

       Scoped to the rail, and nothing else, through NavRailContent's own Classes="tm-nav-rail"
       (MainWindow.axaml) — the class that file kept for exactly this. The descendant combinator
       reaches every one of the five rows on the logical tree, ListBox to ListBoxItem to TextBlock and
       Button to TextBlock alike, which is intended: both apps draw every rail glyph at one contrast.
       tm-icon's own Foreground further up is untouched, so the search affordance, the folder hint and
       the settings cards' icons keep theirs. -->
  <Style Selector="Grid.tm-nav-rail TextBlock.tm-icon">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Round 3, Task 2: live UI Automation on Task Manager's OWN rail found its
       item column is 40 DIP wide inside the SAME 48 DIP pane this app already used — ours
       measured only 32 DIP before this fix. The difference was this ListBox's own horizontal Padding,
       which used to add a SECOND 4 DIP inset on top of the ListBoxItem Margin="4,2" that TmNavRowItem
       sets (Styles.Resources, above), eating 16
       DIP total from the 48 DIP pane (48-16=32) instead of the 8 DIP the item Margin alone accounts for
       (48-8=40). NavRail/NavFooterRail (MainWindow.axaml) now both set Padding with a zero horizontal
       component locally (a local value on the instance, not a class-wide Setter here, since the two
       differ vertically — see their own remarks) to fix the item column to Task Manager's measured 40
       DIP with no other change; row height was already correct and is untouched. -->

  <!-- ══ Rail rows are icon-only, and there is no rule here that makes them so ═══════════════════
       Round 4, item 1 deleted the three rail-state rules that used to live at this spot — one hid every
       row's label TextBlock, the other two centred the icon+label StackPanel inside a ListBoxItem and
       inside a nav-row Button. All three targeted elements that no longer exist: the rail has a single
       width, so a row's whole content is its 16 DIP icon and each icon states its own
       HorizontalAlignment="Center" locally (MainWindow.axaml). Neither the class those selectors keyed
       off nor the label class they targeted is written anywhere in the app any more. Do not re-add a
       state rule here without re-adding the state it describes. -->

  <!-- ══ The footer's one group boundary ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       A single hairline separating the terminal action from the reversible rows. Round 4, item 2 put it
       UNDER 退出, which was then the footer's first row; Round 5, item 1 moved 退出 to the bottom at the
       user's request and the hairline travelled with it, so it now sits ABOVE 退出 and below 设置.
       Neither this rule nor its geometry changed — only its position in MainWindow.axaml, whose footer
       StackPanel carries the ordering rationale and the reason the fence has to follow the row.

       Both numbers are derived, not picked. The HORIZONTAL 4 is the rows' own Margin="4,2" (the
       TmNavRowItem and TmNavRowButton ControlThemes both set it), so the
       line spans exactly the 40 DIP item column — 48 DIP pane − 2×4 DIP item margin — that Round 3
       measured against Task Manager's own rail, and stops flush with the row boxes rather than running
       edge-to-edge across the pane. The VERTICAL 4 puts 4 DIP of clear space on each side of the
       1 DIP line, which with the neighbouring rows' own 2 DIP vertical margins leaves 6 DIP between the
       line and each adjacent row box, against the 4 DIP that separates two ordinary rows: enough to
       read as a boundary, not so much that it reads as a missing row. The whole block is 9 DIP tall
       (1 + 2×4), so the footer measures 173 DIP rather than 164 — four rows at the 40 DIP pitch, plus
       this block, plus the footer's own 4 DIP bottom inset. It was 213 against 204 until Round 5, item 2
       took the 关于 row out of the footer altogether.

       StrokeDividerBrush had NO consumer in this codebase before this rule — it is defined in both
       theme variants (Tokens.axaml) and was referenced nowhere. It is the right token anyway: a
       divider is exactly what this is, and being a DynamicResource it follows the light/dark swap for
       free. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-nav-separator">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="4,4" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeDividerBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The content pane ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ROUND 7, TASK 1 FALSIFIED TWO PREMISES OF THE MEASUREMENT RECORD BELOW. READ THIS FIRST.
         1. The window no longer requests Mica. MainWindow.axaml now asks for AcrylicBlur and this
            machine grants it (log: "Backdrop: requested AcrylicBlur, granted AcrylicBlur"). Every
            sentence below that says "Mica" describes the surface this pane sat on until that commit.
            The Δ-based conclusions may well survive — acrylic is also a mid-tone blur that straddles
            SurfaceBaseBrush rather than reaching SurfacePageBrush's white — but NOBODY HAS
            RE-MEASURED THEM ON ACRYLIC, and this note exists so nobody quotes them as if somebody had.
         2. The search field is no longer on the window's backdrop. It moved out of the title strip and
            back into the 应用 page (Views/AppPage.axaml), where it sits on the WHITE .app pane — so the
            "a settings card on Mica is already on screen; it is the search field" paragraph names a
            control that no longer photographs what it was cited for.
       Round 7's Task 7 owns re-deriving this whole block against Aero and deleting what does not
       survive; Task 1 marks it rather than pre-empting that.

       Round 4, item 5: MainWindow.axaml's PagePane — the Border that hosts whichever page is showing.
       The two rules below are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE by construction (.app against :not(.app)), not a base
       rule plus an override, and that is deliberate: see "WHY THESE LIVE HERE" below.

       Round 6, item 2 flipped which page is the exception. Until then the class was "settings" and the
       painted rule was :not(.settings), i.e. the white bordered pane was the DEFAULT and 设置 was the
       opt-out. The user's sentence is the other way round (除了应用页，其他页面使用和窗口相同的材质面板),
       so the class is now "app": a page that is not 应用 comes up on the window's own material, and a
       hypothetical third page would come up on the material rather than silently white. ShellPage has
       exactly two members today (Services/IShellNavigator.cs), so this changes nothing that is on
       screen except 设置 — it changes which way the default points.

       ── 应用 (the .app rule) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       A pure white pane with a 1 DIP hairline on its TOP and LEFT edges only and an 8 DIP top-left
       corner, flush against the rail and against the caption with zero inset — it is not a floating
       card. Every one of those four facts is measured off the user's reference capture of Task Manager
       (3.png, 1:1 at native resolution, so DIP = px / 1.5). NOTE that no colour below is written as a
       hex literal, in comments included: Fluent2TokenTests.LauncherStyles_ContainNoColourLiterals greps
       this WHOLE FILE, prose and all, and fails the build on one. Decimal RGB triples throughout.

         fill    (255,255,255) at (1700,100) and (100,600) — both re-sampled off 3.png during this
                 implementation                                -> SurfacePageBrush
         border  2 physical px on the top and left edges only, measuring (232,227,233) at x = 74,75
                 y = 600 and (229,228,234) at y = 77,78 x = 400 — the pane fill starts at x = 76 / y = 79.
                 Both are re-sampled here. On the RIGHT edge x = 1916..1921 reads (240,240,240) from
                 y = 400 down but pure white at y = 200, i.e. a scrollbar track and not a border; there
                 is no right or bottom border. StrokeControlBrush is the right token for it without a new
                 one being minted: its Light value is black at alpha 15, and 15/255 = 0.0588 multiplies
                 what is behind it by 0.9412, so over this app's own white pane it predicts
                 255 x 0.9412 = 240 — and the app's border measures exactly (240,240,240). Authored as
                 1 DIP rather than the measured 1.33: 2 physical px IS a 1 DIP stroke layout-rounded at
                 this machine's 150%, and 1.33 would be wrong at every other scale.
         corner  top-left ONLY -> RadiusPaneTopLeft (that token carries the arc measurement, this app's
                 rendered arc beside the reference's, and the reason 8 DIP is the right value for it)
         inset   0 on all four edges; the reference's pane starts at the first pixel after the rail
                 (x = 73 is still backdrop, the border's outer edge is x = 74)

       The 1 DIP left and top borders are laid out INSIDE this Border, so they take 1 DIP each away from
       the page. MainWindow.RefitToContent hands that DIP back through PaneBorderDip, and
       WindowHost.ChromeHeightDip does the same for the top one — see both constants' own remarks. Do
       not change this thickness without changing those.

       ── every other page (the :not(.app) rule) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Paints NOTHING: Transparent, no border, no corner, so the window's own surface — the backdrop
       when the platform grants it, otherwise whatever ApplyBackdrop put on Window.Background — runs
       unbroken from the rail and the caption straight through the content. That is what Windows' own
       Settings does — measured on it during the Round 6 reconnaissance, not assumed: at image y = 470
       its capture reads exactly (243,242,249) at x = 20, 100, 200, 300, 400, 450, 480, 490 and 499,
       nine samples and one value, straight through where a pane edge would be. It has no content pane
       at all; nav column and content column are one continuous surface with no border, no corner and
       no seam.

       ROUND 6, ITEM 2 OVERRULES ROUND 4 HERE — deliberately, on new measurements, not by accident.
       Round 4 gave this half of the pane SurfaceBaseBrush and argued: our cards are SurfaceLayerBrush
       (white at alpha 179), which composites to (251,251,251) over a 243 pane — a Δ8 separation — but
       to (255,255,255) over a WHITE one, Δ0, i.e. a card the literal colour of the page behind it. That
       arithmetic is right, and it is an argument about WHITE. White is not what this pane becomes.
       Card minus backdrop, both terms taken at the same y. FOUR OF THESE SIX ROWS ARE PHOTOGRAPHS and
       two are arithmetic; the rows say which, because they were not all obtained the same way. MEASURED
       rows: the two "over SurfaceBaseBrush" rows are the real 设置 cards on the pre-change build, and the
       two "AFTER" rows are this build, each pair sampled in one capture. COMPUTED rows: before this
       change the 设置 pane was opaque, so no settings card on Mica existed to photograph — those two
       rows composite the card's token alphas over Mica backdrops that WERE measured, at image x = 78 and
       x ≈ 2000 on the pre-change build (Round 6 reconnaissance, r6-material §3.2, whose own heading
       likewise says "Composited"). The paragraph after the table gives the measured card-token-on-Mica
       deltas that do exist.

                                                     fill − backdrop      border − backdrop
         Light, over SurfaceBaseBrush (was)  MEASURED    (8,8,8)             (−14,−14,−14)
         Light, over Mica, window left edge  computed   (12,10,0)            (−14,−14,−15)
         Light, over Mica, window right edge computed    (6,11,7)            (−14,−14,−14)
         Dark,  over SurfaceBaseBrush (was)  MEASURED    (8,8,8)              (16,16,16)
         Light, AFTER, this build            MEASURED   (12,9,4)             (−13,−14,−15)
         Dark,  AFTER, this build            MEASURED    (9,8,6)              (16,16,15)

       Mica under this window measures 238..247 Light and 30..34 Dark, i.e. it STRADDLES SurfaceBaseBrush
       (243 / 32); it is nowhere near SurfacePageBrush (255 / 44). So the card-to-page separation is
       unchanged to within 2-4 levels and the card's outline is unchanged to within 1. Round 4's Δ0 never
       arises. NOTE that the fill's blue delta can legitimately reach 0 near the window's left edge — that
       is the computed left-edge row, and it is a ceiling effect rather than a surprise: Mica measures 255
       blue there, which a white-at-alpha fill cannot exceed. The outline is what draws the card, and it
       is Δ13-16 everywhere. ABSOLUTE RGBs on this surface are NOT portable: PrintWindow composites the DWM
       backdrop itself, so a Mica pane photographs as the blurred wallpaper behind that window's own
       screen position. Only the relationships above are, which is why they are what is written down.

       And that is not a projection. Border.tm-searchfield (above) uses the SAME three tokens as
       Border.tm-card — SurfaceLayerBrush over a 1 DIP StrokeControlBrush at RadiusControl — and already
       sits directly on this window's real Mica in the title strip. Measured there AT REST: Light backdrop
       (244,240,248) gives fill (252,251,253) and border (230,226,233); Dark backdrop (33,31,35) gives
       fill (41,40,42) and border (49,47,51). Predicted from the token alphas: (252,251,253)/(230,226,233)
       and (41,39,42)/(49,47,50) — within 1 level on every channel. A settings card on Mica is already on
       screen; it is the search field. As card-minus-backdrop, the same shape as the table above and
       PHOTOGRAPHED rather than composited: Light fill (8,11,5) and border (−14,−14,−15); Dark fill
       (8,9,7) and border (16,16,16). Those are the two MEASURED card-token-on-Mica rows the table above
       could not supply, and they land inside the same envelope as its computed pair.
       AT REST is load-bearing when re-measuring this: Border.tm-searchfield.focused (above) swaps the
       fill to SurfaceLayerActiveBrush, and the app focuses the field on show, so a capture taken straight
       after a cold start photographs the WRONG token. Dark reads (43,42,44) focused against (41,40,42) at
       rest — a Δ(10,11,9) that looks like a regression and is not. Navigate away and back (or check for
       the 2 px accent underline at the field's bottom edge) before sampling.

       WHAT THIS BUYS, HONESTLY. Two seams disappear. On the PRE-CHANGE build the vertical rail→pane
       step at image x 82 → 83 in Light measured (+5,+2,−12) at y = 150, (+5,+1,−12) at y = 400 and
       (+4,+1,−10) at y = 700 — small per channel, but a straight 780 px line between a neutral grey and
       a tinted gradient — and the horizontal strip→pane step at y 71 → 72 measured (−2,+4,−4) at
       x = 1500. In DARK the same steps were (+2,0,−6)..(+2,0,−7) and (−2,+1,−2): close to invisible, so
       the payoff is almost entirely a Light-theme payoff and this comment says so rather than
       overselling it. On THIS build both steps measure (0,0,0) at every one of those points, in both
       variants.

       Background is Transparent and is never simply omitted. It states the intent where a missing setter
       reads as an oversight, and a null-background Border is not hit-testable in Avalonia, so the gaps
       between settings cards would stop absorbing pointer input.

       THIS PANE DOES NOT PAINT ITS OWN MICA FALLBACK, and must not be given one. When the platform
       refuses Mica, MainWindow.ApplyBackdrop subscribes Window.Background to SurfaceBaseBrush (a
       resource observable, so it follows the theme variant), and everything transparent above it shows
       that colour. A second fallback brush here would be a second source of truth for "what colour is
       this window's surface", and it would be wrong in exactly the case it exists for: the window's
       surface is Window.Background, whatever that resolves to.

       WHAT IS AND IS NOT ESTABLISHED ABOUT THAT FALLBACK. The refused path was NOT exercised. It was
       attempted in full while this item shipped — EnableTransparency cleared in the registry, the
       window hidden and re-summoned so Opened and therefore ApplyBackdrop genuinely re-ran, then an
       ImmersiveColorSet broadcast and a cold restart — and this machine kept granting Mica through all
       three, so ActualTransparencyLevel never left Mica and the branch was never entered. Do not read
       "the fallback works" into this comment. What IS established: the mechanism it relies on is not
       new, because the rail is already Transparent over Window.Background and this pane measured
       continuous with it to (0,0,0) at every sampled point. (That sentence used to cite the title strip
       as the second witness, through TitleBarBackgroundBrush = Transparent in Tokens.axaml. Round 7,
       Task 1 deleted both the strip and that key — the caption is drawn by the window's own decorations
       template now — so only the rail's half of the observation survives.) What the fallback WOULD produce is arithmetic over the tokens,
       computed and not measured: flat (243,243,243) Light / (32,32,32) Dark, with (251,251,251) /
       (40,40,40) cards behind (229,229,229) / (48,48,48) outlines — i.e. the same colours 设置 had
       before this change, when the pane painted SurfaceBaseBrush itself.

       ── WHY THESE LIVE HERE, AND NOT AS LOCAL VALUES ON THE BORDER ─────────────────────────────────
       Because a style setter cannot override a local value in Avalonia, in either direction. A class
       selector's setters are applied at BindingPriority.StyleTrigger, which the framework's own
       documentation on that member describes as overriding a TEMPLATE value; LocalValue is a strictly
       higher priority than both (Avalonia 12.1.0's BindingPriority: Animation, LocalValue, StyleTrigger,
       Template, Style, Inherited, Unset). The app already relies on the same ranking from the other
       side — see the SearchBox TextBox's own remarks in Views/AppPage.axaml, where a LOCAL Background is
       what stops Avalonia's :pointerover style repainting the field. So writing the 应用 look as
       Background/BorderThickness/CornerRadius attributes on PagePane and then trying to switch it with a
       class toggle would silently do nothing at all: the pane would stay white everywhere. Both states
       are therefore styles, and they are written as non-overlapping selectors so the result does not
       depend on this file's rule ORDER either. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-page-pane.app">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfacePageBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1,1,0,0" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusPaneTopLeft}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-page-pane:not(.app)">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ 应用 categories ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NO MaxWidth on EITHER of the two rules below — the panel Border as much as the StackPanel it
       wraps. A cap would only clamp the width the group REPORTS to the horizontal
       StackPanel of categories; the columns ItemsControl inside (AppPage.axaml, bound to
       MenuGroupVm.Columns) keeps laying out however many columns MenuLayout.ChunkIntoColumns
       produced and nothing clips it, so a category with more columns than the cap allows would
       render straight over its right-hand neighbour. Round 4, item 3 removed the tile's fixed width,
       which changes the premise but not the conclusion: a column is as wide as its own widest label,
       so a group's honest desired width is a finite sum of finite column widths — still bounded, and
       still what the scroller needs. Round 6, item 4 added the Border around the StackPanel, and a cap
       there would fail the same way one level further out.

       VerticalAlignment="Top" stays on the StackPanel and is deliberately ABSENT from the Border, and
       the pair is what makes the panels equal height with their content hugging the top. In a
       horizontal StackPanel a child at the default Stretch is arranged to the row's height, so every
       panel Border comes out as tall as the tallest category; the inner StackPanel's Top then keeps
       each heading and tile grid at the top of its own panel and leaves the slack at the bottom of the
       shorter ones. Ragged panels were the alternative and were rejected: measured today the five
       categories are 231.33 / 431.33 / 431.33 / 271.33 / 271.33 DIP, and five bordered boxes of five
       different heights side by side read as an error, where Windows 11 Settings' own large
       side-by-side tiles are equal height. It costs nothing in the fit, because Stretch changes the
       ARRANGE and the panel's DESIRED height is still its own content — WindowHost.FitToContent
       measures the tallest exactly as before. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-group">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Round 6, item 4 (应用页面每个分类增加一个面板样式): each category on the 应用 page sits in a
       panel. This is a deliberate DEPARTURE from Windows 11 rather than a match to it, and saying so is
       the point of this paragraph: the reference draws no container around a labelled group at all —
       measured on ms-settings:privacy, where the three group labels' ink starts at x = 500/500/501
       against their row cards' outer edge at x = 498, i.e. the label sits on the page above a bare
       stack of cards. What the reference DOES supply, and what every number here comes from, is the
       scale of its large side-by-side containers (RadiusPanel's own remarks in Tokens.axaml carry that
       measurement).

       FILL: SubtleFillTertiaryBrush, and the choice was made by measurement on the white 应用 pane, not
       by taste. Three candidates were measured or composited against SurfacePageBrush there:
         SurfaceLayerBrush        renders (255,255,255) — measured; the pane and a tile's interior both
                                  read pure white, so a tm-card-style panel here would be an outline
                                  drawn around tiles that are themselves outlines. Invisible.
         SubtleFillSecondaryBrush renders (245,245,245) — which is EXACTLY the colour a tile turns on
                                  hover (TmTileButton's StateLayer, this file). A panel resting at the
                                  hover colour is a collision, not a surface.
         SubtleFillTertiaryBrush  renders (249,249,249). Delta 6 from the pane, delta 4 from hover, and it
                                  coincides only with a tile's PRESSED wash, which is transient.
       SurfaceBaseBrush was ruled out on sight: (243,243,243) opaque is the grey pane Round 4, item 5
       removed from this page.

       And the panel repairs something the recon measured on the way past. A tile on this page has NO
       fill today — pane and tile interior both (255,255,255), delta 0; it is an outline, not a surface.
       Over a 249 panel the same tile composites to (253,253,253), a real delta 4, while hover keeps its
       present strength (245 over 255 today, 243 over 253 now — delta 10 either way). The two hairlines
       differ too, which is the "not two identical nested outlines" property: StrokeControlBrush over
       the pane's 255 gives the panel's own (240,240,240), over the panel's 249 it gives the tile's
       (234,234,234). Dark is computed from the same alphas and separates MORE, not less: pane
       (44,44,44), panel fill (53,53,53), panel border (59,59,59), tile fill (55,55,55), tile border
       (67,67,67).

       PADDING 12,12,8,8 is a UNIFORM 12 DIP visible inset. The asymmetry corrects for something already
       in the tile grid: every tile carries Margin="0,0,4,4" (AppPage.axaml), so the grid already
       contributes 4 DIP on its own right and bottom inside the group box — measured on the running
       build, where the single-column 常用工具 group reports 122.67 DIP against its tile's 118.67, and
       the 工具 group reports 431.33 = 31.33 + 10 x (36 + 4). A symmetric Padding="12" would render
       12/12/16/16. The 12 is Space3 and is the reference's measured large-tile gutter, which is also
       what AppPage.axaml's categories panel now uses between panels, so the inset and the gutter are
       one number rather than two.

       NOT Border.tm-card, though the anatomy looks similar. tm-card encodes a specific 设置 row — its
       Border must wrap one Auto,*,Auto Grid holding exactly one tm-card-title and one tm-card-sub, and
       SettingsRowTests enforces that over Views/Settings. A category panel has none of those parts, so
       reusing the class would be a semantic collision that passes CI today only because that test's
       CardsDir does not reach Views/AppPage.axaml — one careless widening away from failing four tests
       at once. Its fill does not transfer either, per the measurement above. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-group-panel">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SubtleFillTertiaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusPanel}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,12,8,8" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ 设置 cards ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Windows 11 Settings' row card: a layered surface with a hairline outline, an icon, a title +
       secondary line, and the control itself trailing. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.tm-card">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,12" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,4" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Round 5, item 3: LineHeight was the one half of the type ramp these two classes skipped, and
       they were the ONLY text classes in this file that skipped it — tm-group-head and tm-page-title
       below both set theirs. Measured cost of the omission on the live page: a title line box of
       19.33 DIP instead of the token's 20, a subtitle box of 16.67 instead of 16, and 0.67 DIP of
       drift WITHIN the subtitle class (the two pure-ASCII path lines measured 16.0 while every
       subtitle containing CJK measured 16.67). Setting both pins a title-over-subtitle header block at
       exactly 20 + 16 = 36 DIP, which is what lets one VerticalAlignment="Center" put the icon and the
       trailing control at the same y on every row that HAS such a block. When this was written three
       rows did not: PinTaskbarTitle and HotkeyTitle (BehaviorCard) and UpdateStatusText (UpdatesCard)
       were bare tm-card-titles in column 1 with no tm-card-sub beside them, so their header block
       measured 20 DIP, not 36. Part B gave all three a real two-line header, and
       SettingsRowTests.PendingTwoLineHeader — which listed exactly those three — is now empty. Every
       row on the page has the block.

       WHAT IT COSTS IN CARD HEIGHT — it is not free, and one card GREW. Measured before and after on
       the same window at 150 %, each card scrolled fully into view first and read from the card
       Border's own UIA rect (physical px), over the sixteen cards the page had at the time (part B
       deleted one of them): twelve did not change, three
       lost 1-2 px and one gained 1. Two effects cancel on a conforming row — the title gains 0.67 and
       a CJK subtitle loses 0.67 — and a row whose column 2 holds a 32 DIP button or ToggleSwitch
       absorbs the title's gain outright, because that grid row was already the control's height.
       (The ToggleSwitch measured 32 DIP tall when this was written; Round 7, Task 3's Aero pill made
       it 34, which changes none of the card heights below — re-read on the same instrument, the four
       toggle rows still sit exactly 100 physical px apart, because 34 is still under the 36 DIP
       header block that sets the row.) The
       SIGN of every delta follows from those two; the exact pixel counts do not reproduce from the
       line-box arithmetic alone, because each UIA rect is a separately rounded pair of doubles at 1.5x,
       so they are quoted as measured. Three cards lost: PinTaskbarStatusText's card 125 -> 124 and
       HotkeyHelpText's 126 -> 125 (one lone CJK line below the Grid each, title gain absorbed by the
       control in column 2), and DataFolderTitle's 219 -> 217 (two CJK lines against one title, around
       an unchanged ASCII path line). One card gained: UpdatesCard's status card, 132 -> 133 — a bare
       title, no subtitle to cancel it, and an empty column 2, so nothing absorbs it. One physical pixel
       on the one card part B rebuilt from the header down (it gained a real title, demoted
       UpdateStatusText to the subtitle and moved its buttons into column 2), so it was recorded here
       rather than chased. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-card-title">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightBody}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-card-sub">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeCaption}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightCaption}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <!-- Wrap rather than overflow: the middle column is whatever is left after the leading icon and
         the trailing control, which is narrower than several of these descriptions' natural width. -->
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ══ The row spec's shared classes ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 5, item 3 (设置页每个设置项统一设计规范). One anatomy for every settings row: a 16 DIP icon,
       a header of EXACTLY one title line and one subtitle line, the control trailing in column 2, and
       an optional expansion row starting at column 1's own origin. Everything in that anatomy which
       has an AvaloniaProperty behind it is a class here.

       ONE PART OF THE ANATOMY CANNOT BE A STYLE. Grid exposes ShowGridLinesProperty, RowSpacingProperty,
       ColumnSpacingProperty, ColumnProperty, RowProperty, ColumnSpanProperty, RowSpanProperty and
       IsSharedSizeScopeProperty — and no ColumnDefinitionsProperty or RowDefinitionsProperty at all
       (checked by reflection over the shipped Avalonia.Controls.dll 12.1.0). ColumnDefinitions and
       RowDefinitions are plain CLR properties, and a Setter needs an AvaloniaProperty, so
       ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" is written literally in every card and guarded by a source test
       instead (tests/TanMenu.Core.Tests/Themes/SettingsRowTests.cs).

       RowSpacing IS a StyledProperty and could have carried the header-to-expansion gap. It deliberately
       does not: a row gap is charged between row definitions even when the expansion row's only child is
       IsVisible="False" (UpdatesCard's ProgressBar), which would leave 8 DIP of dead space on that card
       in its normal state. The gap lives on the expansion child's own Margin instead (tm-card-expander),
       and an invisible child contributes no margin at all.

       These rules are declared AFTER the two they refine, the same ordering this file already relies on
       for TextBlock.tm-icon.subtle overriding TextBlock.tm-icon's Foreground. -->

  <!-- The icon box. :is(Control) rather than a TextBlock-scoped selector so the same class boxes a row
       whose leading mark is not a glyph without a second rule — which part C then made real: the 关于
       card's mark is the product tile, an Image (Views/Settings/AboutCard.axaml).
       Width 16 fixes the column independently of the glyph: all fifteen
       distinct glyphs the settings page carried when this was measured already measured exactly 16 DIP
       wide (fourteen since part B deleted the 当前版本 card and with it the page's only E946), so this
       changes nothing and stops a future glyph from moving the title. Height is deliberately NOT set — the 16 DIP box is
       Segoe Fluent Icons' own line box at FontSize 16, and pinning a height would clip the glyph if the
       Windows 10 fallback face (Segoe MDL2 Assets) measures taller. An Image has no line box to supply
       one, so that ONE row states Height="16" locally; every glyph row still must not. -->
  <Style Selector=":is(Control).tm-card-icon">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="16" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,14,0" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The header text column: title over subtitle, nothing else. 12 is the gutter to the trailing
       control; Center is what pairs with the icon's own Center so both land on one y once the header is
       always two lines. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-text">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,12,0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A value line — a path, a size, a version: a subtitle that is one ellipsized line rather than a
       wrapping paragraph. Used WITH tm-card-sub (Classes="tm-card-sub tm-card-value") so it inherits
       size, colour and line height and states only the three things that differ.

       THERE IS NO MaxWidth HERE ANY MORE, AND RE-ADDING ONE NEEDS THIS PARAGRAPH READ FIRST. The class
       shipped with MaxWidth="260" — a MEASURE-time bound, on the argument that a NoWrap +
       CharacterEllipsis TextBlock reports its full untrimmed single-line advance as its desired width
       (trimming is a paint-time concern), so one long path would inflate the card's desired width and
       push the trailing controls past the visible edge. That mechanism was measured when a path line
       lived in column 1, BESIDE those controls. Round 5 moved every value line onto a Grid.Row="1"
       ColumnSpan="2" expansion row, and it does not reach there — re-measured rather than reasoned:
       with the Setter deleted and DataPathText fed a ~250-character path still ellipsized at 1182.00 DIP
       on the widest row this page has — so at least twice the row it sits on below — at the app's own
       floor (MainWindow's MinWidth="760"; SetWindowPos
       clamps at GetWindowRect 1162 px = 760 DIP client, card 664.00 DIP, content box 629.33, expansion
       span 599.33) in BOTH languages: every card still measures 664.00 DIP with unchanged heights, every
       trailing control still lands on the content right edge (0.00 DIP over; 12.00 on the five toggle
       rows, the documented tm-card-toggle overhang), and SettingsScroll still reports
       HorizontallyScrollable=False with a vertical ScrollBar as its only one. It cannot reach there:
       SettingsPage's ScrollViewer never enables horizontal scrolling, so ScrollContentPresenter measures
       CardStack against the real viewport width, the card Border stretches to it, and a spanning child's
       desired width has nowhere to inflate into.

       What the bound did cost was visible: DataPathText sat clamped at 259.33 DIP and already ellipsized
       (258 DIP of ink) on a row measured at 1185.33 DIP at the app's fitted width — some 925 DIP of
       empty space immediately right of a truncated path. That same real path measures 522.67 DIP
       untrimmed and now renders whole. It still ellipsizes when the row genuinely runs out (591.33 DIP
       of the probe path at the floor), which is what the trimming is for.

       HorizontalAlignment="Left" STAYS, but its job changed with the bound's removal. It is no longer
       what stops the accidental centring — Avalonia 12.1.0's Layoutable.ArrangeCore clamps the arranged
       width to MaxWidth and then runs the Stretch case THROUGH Center's centring arithmetic (the two
       labels share one switch case), so a MaxWidth with no stated alignment centres the line, which is
       exactly what put the two folder rows' path lines 384.67 and 456.67 DIP right of their own titles
       on the shipped build. With no MaxWidth there is nothing to centre. What Left does now is keep the
       element's bounds equal to its TEXT: a Stretch line arranges to the whole span instead — measured
       against UpdateHelpText, a plain tm-card-sub on the same kind of row, which reports 1185.33 DIP
       fitted and 599.33 at the floor, the full span both times — which would spread the two path lines'
       ToolTip across the empty width beside them. The centring mechanism is separately load-bearing in
       SettingsPage.axaml, which uses it deliberately to centre CardStack at its own cap.

       THE ToolTip RULE SURVIVES THE BOUND, restated against the row rather than against 260: a value
       line whose text CAN outgrow the row it sits on and has no ToolTip is a defect in that card, and
       supplying one is the CONSUMER'S job, not this class's. FoldersCard's two path lines are unbounded
       user data — a long one still ellipsizes at the floor — and set a matching ToolTip in code
       (FoldersCard.axaml.cs:102, 138, 220). Its third value line, DataInfoText, sets none on purpose:
       its text is the fixed CurrentDataSize / CurrentDataPending format over one of four short kind
       literals plus a formatted byte count, and it measures 124.67 DIP Chinese / 142.00 English against
       the floor's 599.33 span, so it can never be trimmed and a ToolTip would only repeat the line
       already on screen. AboutCard's two lines (91.33 and 186.00) go without one for the same reason. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-card-value">
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="NoWrap" />
    <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The expansion row's own top gap, on the child rather than on the Grid — see the RowSpacing note
       above. 8 rather than the 10 three of the old below-row blocks stated: 8 is a step on the spacing
       ramp and 10 is not, and the two below-row TEXT margins the page already had were 8. Part B put
       every action strip in column 2, so what this class now spaces is full-width expansion content —
       FoldersCard's value lines, ToolsCard's bordered list, UpdatesCard's help line and progress bar. -->
  <Style Selector=":is(Control).tm-card-expander">
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,8,0,0" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-expander">
    <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="8" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Value lines stack tighter than blocks do. Declared after tm-card-expander so a panel carrying both
       classes resolves to 2: at equal specificity the later rule wins, and a stack OF value lines is
       what the smaller gap is for. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-values">
    <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="2" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Every action strip on the page. Part B put them all in column 2, however many buttons they hold:
       the old "three or more go below" rule rested on a 760 DIP overflow that was re-measured once the
       value lines left column 1, and no longer exists (see FoldersCard's class remark for the numbers).
       HorizontalAlignment="Right" is therefore inert today — column 2 is Auto-sized to the strip — and
       is kept because it is what would put a full-width expansion-row strip on the content edge, so the
       one class satisfies "every control's right edge is the card's" in either position.
       VerticalAlignment="Center" on the strip is what makes the per-button VerticalAlignment attributes
       unnecessary: a horizontal StackPanel stretches its children to its own height. -->
  <Style Selector="StackPanel.tm-card-actions">
    <Setter Property="Orientation" Value="Horizontal" />
    <Setter Property="Spacing" Value="8" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The settings toggle. Blanking OnContent/OffContent moves out of the cards that each repeat it:
       Avalonia's default is the untranslated English "On"/"Off", which would sit next to a Chinese row
       label whenever AppLanguage is zh-Hans.

       THE NEGATIVE RIGHT MARGIN IS NOT SLOP — do not remove it without re-reading this. The template
       behind this class builds an inner Grid of three columns: Auto for the pill, then a hard 12 pixel
       column with MaxWidth 12, then Auto for the two content presenters. That fixed spacer is charged
       whether or not the presenters are empty, so blanking the content collapses column 3 and leaves
       column 2: the element measures 12 DIP wider than its visible pill, and the pill's ink stopped
       12 DIP short of every other row's control on the build before this class existed. A
       ColumnDefinition is not a Control, so no /template/ selector can reach it. The margin makes the
       desired width the pill's own, so the Auto column measures the pill and ArrangeCore deflates the
       slot by the negative margin — the pill lands on the content edge and the 12 DIP of empty presenter
       overhangs into the card's own 16 DIP right padding, where it draws nothing.

       Round 7, Task 3 replaced the template underneath and the margin SURVIVED UNCHANGED, because the
       vendored Aero ToggleSwitch copies that inner grid verbatim, `ColumnDefinition Width="12"
       MaxWidth="12"` included (Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/ToggleSwitch.axaml) — identical to Fluent
       12.1.0's, decompiled. The pill itself did change, and every number below is a fresh UI Automation
       BoundingRectangle read on the live 设置 page at this machine's 150 % scale, on the same four
       toggles, on a build with this task's changes and a build without:

         element bounds   78 x 48 physical  ->  102 x 51   (52 x 32 DIP -> 68 x 34)
         pill             40 x 20 DIP       ->   56 x 22   (VENDORED Width/Height/CornerRadius 10)
         element LEFT     x = 4739 physical ->  x = 4715   (16 DIP further left, as the pill grew)
         element RIGHT    x = 4817 physical ->  x = 4817   (UNCHANGED)

       The right edge is the one that matters and it did not move: 4817 minus the 12 DIP (18 physical)
       of empty presenter is 4799, which is the same screen column as the right edge of every TmButton
       on the page — PinTaskbarBtn, HotkeyClearBtn, PickRootBtn and RestoreBtn all end at 4799 in the
       same capture. If a future Avalonia or a future vendored file changes that column, the toggle rows
       shift by whatever the change is. -->
  <Style Selector="ToggleSwitch.tm-card-toggle">
    <Setter Property="OnContent" Value="" />
    <Setter Property="OffContent" Value="" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,-12,0" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The hotkey capture box (BehaviorCard's HotkeyBox), and the reason this class exists at all: it was
       the ONE control on the 设置 page carrying no Classes and no Theme, so it fell through to stock
       Fluent in every state. The visible consequence was its DISABLED fill. Fluent's TextBox
       ControlTheme repaints Border#PART_BorderElement's Background to TextControlBackgroundDisabled ->
       SystemControlBackgroundBaseLowBrush -> SystemBaseLowColor, which is 20% black; over the card that
       measures (201,201,201), against the (253,253,253) every disabled TmButton on the same page
       measures. Two disabled vocabularies 50 RGB levels apart on one page — the heavy grey box the user
       screenshotted.

       The box IS legitimately disabled whenever 启用全局热键 is off (BehaviorCard's
       UpdateHotkeyEnabledState), and that logic is correct and untouched. Only its paint changes.

       Targeting the named template part is what reaches the screen: an app-level Style outranks a
       ControlTheme's own rule for the same part at the same pseudo-class — the precedence written up in
       full at TextBox.tm-search:focus above, which beats Fluent's built-in focus repaint the same way.
       The :disabled recipe is deliberately TmButton's own (TextDisabledBrush plus Opacity 0.6 over the
       resting surface), so the page has one disabled vocabulary rather than two.

       The focus state is not decoration here. "Click the box, then press the combination" IS the
       interaction (the tunneling KeyDown handler in BehaviorCard's ctor), so the box has to say when it
       is listening. One accent hairline, not Fluent's filled accent band — the same conclusion Round 3,
       Task 1 reached for the search field. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-keycap">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="160" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
    <!-- Vertical padding 0, exactly as TmButton states it, and for the same reason: the height then
         comes from MinHeight rather than from font metrics plus padding, so the box matches the button
         standing beside it in the same action strip. The card's inherited Padding="8,5" did not — it
         put the box's own content over 32 DIP, so MinHeight stopped binding. Measured on the live page
         at 150 %, reading the box's UIA rect at each of the eight scroll stops: with 8,5 it was
         50/50/49/48/49/49 physical px against a TmButton's flat 48 at every stop, and because a
         horizontal StackPanel stretches its children to its own height, 清除 was stretched to the same
         50. With 8,0 it is 49/48/48/47/48/48 — the same value as the button at every stop but one, and
         the ±1 that remains is rect quantisation, not layout: it tracks the scroll offset, and at the
         same stops this card's title and subtitle read 29-30 and 24-25 for a box that never changes.
         Setting an explicit Height instead of MinHeight was tried and measured byte-for-byte identical,
         so MinHeight stays — it is what TmButton uses and it cannot clip a taller font fallback.
         VerticalContentAlignment centres the text in whatever height MinHeight settles on. -->
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,0" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-keycap /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-keycap:pointerover /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerActiveBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-keycap:focus /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerActiveBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentDefaultBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-keycap:disabled">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.6" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBox.tm-keycap:disabled /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLayerBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StrokeControlBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Round 3, Task 3 (user item 9): the actual PAGE title ("设置") and each card GROUP'S own heading
       ("外观"/"行为"/"文件夹"/"常用工具"/"更新") both used to share this one class at FontSizeSubtitle
       (20) — the page title was too close in size to its own subsections. Live-measured Task Manager's
       settings page (ink-height pixel sampling, not automation bounding boxes — those
       include per-row template padding that differs between its title and its section rows and would
       have overstated the title's real size by nearly 3x) found its page title's ink is ~1.4x its
       section headings' ink, while its section headings and its CARD titles measure the SAME ink height
       — the two are told apart by weight (bold vs regular) alone, confirmed by both ink height and ink
       density (stroke-darkness-per-area, a boldness proxy) on live pixels. Our own ramp already has
       FontSizeSubtitle:FontSizeBody = 20:14 = 1.43, matching that measured 1.4x almost exactly, so the
       fix is a straight remap rather than a new token: the real page title gets its own class
       (tm-page-title, below) at the old Subtitle size, and this class — now used only for the five
       card-group headings — drops to FontSizeBody, SemiBold, matching Task Manager's own section
       headings sitting at card-title size but bolder. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-group-head">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightBody}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,20,0,8" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The one real page title (SettingsPage.axaml's own PageTitle) — AppPage's own
       "tm-empty > TextBlock.title" (above) already uses this exact Subtitle+SemiBold shape for its own
       single big heading, so this reuses the established size rather than inventing one. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.tm-page-title">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeSubtitle}" />
    <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="{DynamicResource LineHeightSubtitle}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
  </Style>

</Styles>
